	abstract = {ABSTRACT
            
              
                
                  To effectively monitor biodiversity in streams and rivers, we need to quantify species distribution accurately.
                
                
                  Occupancy models are useful for distinguishing between the non‐detection of a species and its actual absence. While these models can account for spatial autocorrelation, they are not suited for streams and rivers due to their unique network spatial structure.
                
                
                  Here, I propose spatial occupancy models specifically designed for data collected on stream and river networks. I present the statistical developments and illustrate their application using data on a semi‐aquatic mammal.
                
                
